    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we will calculate the total number of outcomes when rolling four fair 6-sided dice and then find the number of outcomes where all four dice show the same number. The probability of this happening will be subtracted from 1 to find the probability that they won't all show the same number.\n\n1. **Total number of outcomes when rolling four dice:**\n   Each die has 6 faces, so the total number of possible outcomes when rolling four dice is:\n   \\[\n   6^4 = 1296\n   \\]\n\n2. **Number of outcomes where all four dice show the same number:**\n   There are 6 possible outcomes where all four dice show the same number (one for each face of the die: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, or 6).\n   \n3. **Probability that all four dice show the same number:**\n   The probability is the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number of outcomes:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{6}{1296} = \\frac{1}{216}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Probability that not all four dice show the same number:**\n   This is the complement of the probability that all four dice show the same number:\n   \\[\n   1 - \\frac{1}{216} = \\frac{216}{216} - \\frac{1}{216} = \\frac{215}{216}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that when we roll four fair 6-sided dice, they won't all show the same number is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{215}{216}}\n\\]",
